d/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aae46a7-7405-5484-9202-26c3555ea95d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107832" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z}}{t{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e75041c-d0be-5db2-a0db-7c2c2b57b44e" :knowledge-point/title "Defining the Gamma Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef308e06-992f-56cb-a814-cd6e2eabff6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474230} {:answer/id #uuid "2b21cf5a-1e34-520e-9b32-cc50912a07a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860454